WEBVTT

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:03.200
If we could recite the Pledge of Allegiance, please.

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:05.200
I pledge allegiance

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:06.560
to the flag. The

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:09.200
>> United States Of America

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:11.840
and to the Republic for stands. Which

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:13.760
One nation under

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:15.360
God,

00:00:16.035 --> 00:00:17.235
indivisible,

00:00:17.555 --> 00:00:18.435
with liberty

00:00:18.915 --> 00:00:20.275
and justice for all.

00:00:20.915 --> 00:00:21.795
>> Okay,

00:00:21.795 --> 00:00:22.515
thank you.

00:00:24.595 --> 00:00:29.555
Our first agenda item is public speaking. Do we have any public speak?

00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:38.830
Looks like done, thank you, we have any additions or deletions of the agenda.

00:00:42.110 --> 00:00:46.190
Also hearing none I guess we'll move on

00:00:44.935 --> 00:00:47.015
Town administrators report.

00:00:47.895 --> 00:00:49.175
We're get Eric back.

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:05.060
>> Is there something we can bounce forward to and come back to Eric?

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:08.340
>> Yeah, I think we

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:19.195
can just take our agenda items out of order. Although technically we need a motion maybe to do that. Oh, here's Eric. You're

00:01:19.195 --> 00:01:21.035
on mute. Buddy. You're muted.

00:01:24.155 --> 00:01:29.460
>> Sorry about that. Just trying to get some last minute stuff done before the meeting. Yeah.

00:01:30.980 --> 00:01:35.780
>> Well, we're, we're at item agenda item number four, which is town administrators report.

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:37.220
>> Okay.

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:38.900
Let me, share my screen here.

00:01:55.925 --> 00:01:59.100
Alright. Are you guys all seeing that? Yep. Okay

00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:02.460
first off I just wanted to say

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:07.260
unfortunately last week one of the Andover firefighters

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:10.220
passed away of a heart attack his name was

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:13.420
Gary Calvi or Calvo sorry.

00:02:14.725 --> 00:02:17.605
If any of you want to contribute,

00:02:18.085 --> 00:02:19.285
there is a

00:02:19.445 --> 00:02:30.450
GoFundMe campaign to help pay for his funeral expenses. You can find the link at the Andover support group. If you don't have it and you want it, you can also get me offline.

00:02:31.330 --> 00:02:39.010
The other really good news, actually this is a day out of date, is that the first delivery of vaccine in the state of Connecticut was yesterday.

00:02:39.585 --> 00:02:44.545
So hopefully we are slowly starting to see the tail end of

00:02:45.425 --> 00:02:48.545
what we've been going through for the past eight to ten months.

00:02:48.865 --> 00:02:51.265
That's the good news. Here's the bad news.

00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:55.710
As of today, we've had sixty three positive COVID cases,

00:02:56.430 --> 00:02:57.070
in

00:02:57.470 --> 00:02:59.470
one death in the town.

00:03:00.190 --> 00:03:02.750
When I talked to you at the November meeting,

00:03:02.990 --> 00:03:04.350
we had had 29.

00:03:04.955 --> 00:03:08.955
So we've gone from 29 to sixty three in

00:03:08.955 --> 00:03:09.995
a month.

00:03:10.795 --> 00:03:14.715
I know we've talked a lot in town about the positivity rate.

00:03:15.035 --> 00:03:17.355
This is our fourteen day

00:03:17.675 --> 00:03:19.115
or

00:03:18.490 --> 00:03:19.210
correction,

00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:21.450
our infectivity rate.

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:27.370
If you could look at it, the last time we met was back around the seventeenth or eighteenth,

00:03:27.610 --> 00:03:31.530
and we were at 24 and as of today, we're about seventy one.

00:03:32.175 --> 00:03:33.615
So we're obviously

00:03:34.015 --> 00:03:35.295
not progressing

00:03:35.375 --> 00:03:37.695
in a good direction at the moment.

00:03:39.935 --> 00:03:40.735
We're

00:03:40.975 --> 00:03:46.095
pretty much right now at about the national and Connecticut average for COVID infections.

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:49.050
We had been lagging behind for a long time,

00:03:49.610 --> 00:03:55.370
but in the first fifteen days of this month, we've had thirty new infections in town,

00:03:55.850 --> 00:03:57.770
which is a bummer,

00:03:57.770 --> 00:03:58.250
obviously.

00:04:00.175 --> 00:04:02.415
So I'm not going to show you these graphs.

00:04:02.655 --> 00:04:05.375
The other thing that's been of concern is,

00:04:06.895 --> 00:04:10.495
know, we've gone from in basically two months

00:04:10.735 --> 00:04:16.160
from one hundred and ten to twelve hundred people hospitalized in the state of Connecticut.

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:22.240
So far over the last week and a half or so, that's really leveled off, which is good.

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:24.400
Nobody knows exactly why.

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:31.385
There's no expectation that it's continued to be leveled off because the number of infections keep increasing

00:04:31.705 --> 00:04:34.345
and the number of people hospitalized

00:04:34.505 --> 00:04:39.065
tracks almost perfectly, but delayed about a week or two from that number.

00:04:39.625 --> 00:04:42.185
So anticipate that number to go up.

00:04:42.585 --> 00:04:44.505
The governor has

00:04:46.490 --> 00:04:50.970
announced just recently that they're going to open the Hartford Convention Center

00:04:51.050 --> 00:04:53.690
as a COVID overflow treatment center.

00:04:53.770 --> 00:04:56.890
So if you were planning on going to any conventions

00:04:57.525 --> 00:05:01.685
in the next couple of months, I would say that's probably not a starter.

00:05:03.685 --> 00:05:13.910
We did get a rebate from the state for our COVID related expenses up to this point, and it was right around $7,000

00:05:14.470 --> 00:05:15.590
So

00:05:15.590 --> 00:05:17.750
basically what I'm going to do is

00:05:18.550 --> 00:05:22.630
refund the library their share of the COVID related expenses

00:05:22.950 --> 00:05:25.350
and utilize the rest at the town level.

00:05:26.075 --> 00:05:29.915
Now in addition to that, we just got announced in fact,

00:05:30.475 --> 00:05:35.035
I was just on a conference call just before that when they were giving out the details.

00:05:35.595 --> 00:05:37.435
There will be an initial

00:05:37.435 --> 00:05:40.955
allotment of COVID rebate money for the towns.

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:46.160
Andover's allotment will be about $24,650

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:47.600
which is pretty cool.

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:53.360
I've got about a day to jump through the hoops and fill out all the paperwork.

00:05:55.280 --> 00:05:55.920
And

00:05:56.495 --> 00:05:57.855
we have to

00:06:02.735 --> 00:06:04.655
have earmarked the money

00:06:04.895 --> 00:06:06.135
by December

00:06:06.135 --> 00:06:06.975
31,

00:06:08.495 --> 00:06:10.575
which is somewhat

00:06:12.010 --> 00:06:13.050
unrealistic.

00:06:13.050 --> 00:06:17.370
So what the state has said is they've given towns the option

00:06:17.690 --> 00:06:19.290
of putting that money

00:06:19.530 --> 00:06:21.530
towards public safety.

00:06:21.690 --> 00:06:24.730
They said you can put the money in the general fund,

00:06:24.970 --> 00:06:26.010
but allocated

00:06:26.555 --> 00:06:29.435
to offset the costs for public safety

00:06:29.595 --> 00:06:43.660
and then spend the money you had allocated for public safety on other things. So it's a little bit of a paper trail but the good news is we're gonna get an infusion of cash of about $25,000,

00:06:44.380 --> 00:06:45.900
in the next,

00:06:46.380 --> 00:06:47.340
two weeks.

00:06:47.980 --> 00:06:49.420
So that's kinda cool.

00:06:50.140 --> 00:06:51.900
Always a good thing to get free money.

00:06:52.745 --> 00:06:57.465
>> Is that just an allocation based on population or how is that governed,

00:06:57.705 --> 00:06:58.985
or determined,

00:06:58.985 --> 00:07:02.425
>> Eric? So there's this wacky three part formula.

00:07:03.385 --> 00:07:05.145
It's partly population,

00:07:05.625 --> 00:07:07.865
it's partly population density,

00:07:08.480 --> 00:07:10.000
and it's partly

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:14.320
the grand list divided by the population.

00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:19.760
So they use three different factors and they weighed it and they have this really complex formula.

00:07:20.575 --> 00:07:22.895
So basically relatively speaking,

00:07:23.135 --> 00:07:26.335
smaller more affluent towns such as Andover

00:07:26.975 --> 00:07:28.575
get a disproportionately

00:07:28.575 --> 00:07:30.975
small amount of the total funding

00:07:31.695 --> 00:07:33.135
based on our population,

00:07:33.710 --> 00:07:35.630
but it's still $24,000

00:07:35.630 --> 00:07:37.150
or $25,000.

00:07:37.150 --> 00:07:42.430
So it's still a good shot in the arm. And this is federal money that was given to the state.

00:07:42.910 --> 00:07:43.550
And

00:07:44.110 --> 00:07:45.790
basically what happened is

00:07:47.755 --> 00:07:49.195
many months ago,

00:07:49.355 --> 00:07:52.075
the state asked the towns

00:07:52.155 --> 00:07:55.995
to tell them roughly what their COVID related expenses were,

00:07:56.235 --> 00:07:59.755
but they did it without telling the towns what they could actually credit.

00:08:00.440 --> 00:08:01.640
So towns

00:08:01.640 --> 00:08:02.920
credited everything,

00:08:03.240 --> 00:08:13.480
gave them a number, the money got allocated from the federal government and then the state came back and said, oh, well you can't claim that, that, that, that, that, you can only claim this and this and this,

00:08:14.045 --> 00:08:16.605
which added up to a much smaller number

00:08:16.925 --> 00:08:19.085
than they had money for.

00:08:19.565 --> 00:08:21.725
So that was the $7,000

00:08:21.725 --> 00:08:27.085
even though we originally said we were going to have expenses of around, you know, dollars 18,000.

00:08:29.730 --> 00:08:42.130
So now they've gone back and taken all that extra money because they got to do something with it and they're just basically giving it to the towns but the towns have to certify they're putting it into certain specific categories.

00:08:42.535 --> 00:08:45.575
So we're going be able to do that it's going to be some paperwork

00:08:45.735 --> 00:08:48.535
a little bit of running around by myself and the

00:08:48.775 --> 00:08:52.295
Assistant Finance Director but not too big a deal

00:08:52.695 --> 00:08:53.175
either way.

00:08:55.030 --> 00:08:56.550
So that is

00:08:57.190 --> 00:08:58.310
going on.

00:09:00.070 --> 00:09:00.710
So

00:09:01.030 --> 00:09:07.670
what else? The second thing is that we are going to have to deal with some expenses

00:09:07.750 --> 00:09:08.550
related

00:09:08.550 --> 00:09:09.830
to public works.

00:09:10.335 --> 00:09:12.735
As a lot of you know Ed Kasich

00:09:12.735 --> 00:09:15.135
retired as the road crew foreman.

00:09:15.615 --> 00:09:19.215
He was the road crew foreman for thirty three years,

00:09:19.775 --> 00:09:20.975
so which is a long time.

00:09:24.020 --> 00:09:29.220
I'm happy that he was able you know to retire and was in good health.

00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:42.355
His replacement Jay Tuttle starts officially on the twenty first but he's actually in the garage right now prepping for the storm. So he agreed to come in today because I kind of told him I expected him to.

00:09:42.915 --> 00:09:47.715
And, he said, okay. So we are switching that position from,

00:09:48.755 --> 00:09:51.315
Ed was a union member

00:09:51.810 --> 00:09:56.770
and we are replacing that with a non union

00:09:56.930 --> 00:09:59.010
but working supervisor

00:09:59.170 --> 00:09:59.970
position.

00:10:00.370 --> 00:10:04.130
So we're changing the job title, we're changing the job, you know,

00:10:06.115 --> 00:10:07.075
description

00:10:07.715 --> 00:10:11.795
to make it more in line with what we really need to

00:10:12.035 --> 00:10:12.675
to,

00:10:12.915 --> 00:10:16.275
you know, kind of get in and accomplish at the town.

00:10:16.755 --> 00:10:21.030
So there's two issues with that. The first is because

00:10:21.030 --> 00:10:22.390
Ed had

00:10:23.670 --> 00:10:25.510
maxed out his sick days

00:10:25.750 --> 00:10:28.070
at over 300 and

00:10:28.870 --> 00:10:31.350
in keeping with the agreement that

00:10:32.150 --> 00:10:33.030
we

00:10:33.190 --> 00:10:37.315
had with him that if he would retire, we would give him,

00:10:37.635 --> 00:10:41.235
you know, we would credit him with more of his sick days

00:10:41.555 --> 00:10:42.915
than he

00:10:43.315 --> 00:10:46.115
ordinarily would be allowed by union contract.

00:10:46.355 --> 00:10:49.715
We depleted the severance fund pretty significantly.

00:10:50.330 --> 00:10:53.210
Plus knowing he was going to retire,

00:10:53.530 --> 00:11:00.970
he didn't take any real vacation this year. So per union contract, we owe him out for his unused vacation

00:11:00.970 --> 00:11:05.370
as well as a significant fraction of his unused sick days.

00:11:10.705 --> 00:11:15.345
So that will have an impact that we will have to deal with in the next budget cycle.

00:11:15.745 --> 00:11:24.140
The good news is that there's really only one other employee out there that the town would have any kind of significant exposure

00:11:24.380 --> 00:11:25.980
if they retired

00:11:26.620 --> 00:11:28.300
in terms of severance.

00:11:29.340 --> 00:11:33.580
And that is Tim, Tim Higley, who's one of the other public works employees.

00:11:34.325 --> 00:11:36.405
So if Tim Higley retired,

00:11:36.725 --> 00:11:37.685
we

00:11:37.685 --> 00:11:40.085
would owe somewhere in the $8,000

00:11:40.085 --> 00:11:40.805
range

00:11:41.205 --> 00:11:44.565
for severance on Ed or on Tim.

00:11:45.605 --> 00:11:47.845
So the second thing is because

00:11:49.480 --> 00:11:53.240
we hired Jay Tuttle as a salaried employee,

00:11:54.120 --> 00:11:56.360
as opposed to an hourly employee,

00:11:57.160 --> 00:12:01.080
that's going to do two things. One, it does not affect the overtime

00:12:01.455 --> 00:12:04.415
or the overtime snow removal budget,

00:12:04.655 --> 00:12:06.495
but it does increase

00:12:06.815 --> 00:12:10.415
the amount that will be spent out of the public works

00:12:11.615 --> 00:12:13.615
general salary line item.

00:12:13.855 --> 00:12:16.815
So we're going to be somewhat under budgeted

00:12:17.070 --> 00:12:18.030
on that.

00:12:19.230 --> 00:12:20.430
As of today,

00:12:20.990 --> 00:12:24.510
we are overspent in OT for public works

00:12:24.670 --> 00:12:26.590
and we're overspent

00:12:26.590 --> 00:12:28.510
in the part time help

00:12:28.750 --> 00:12:29.710
category.

00:12:30.675 --> 00:12:33.555
The part time help category was because

00:12:33.795 --> 00:12:36.915
we I employed Richard Bagan for much

00:12:37.155 --> 00:12:37.955
longer

00:12:38.195 --> 00:12:38.915
than

00:12:39.075 --> 00:12:40.915
we had funding for,

00:12:41.155 --> 00:12:46.040
but I did it because I basically plugged him into the fourth public works employee

00:12:46.280 --> 00:12:47.320
category

00:12:48.040 --> 00:12:49.880
instead of because

00:12:50.120 --> 00:12:54.840
the board of selectmen was not going to allow me to hire a

00:12:54.840 --> 00:12:58.600
fourth person until we actually had a retirement date for Eddie.

00:12:59.015 --> 00:13:01.735
So I used him essentially as that

00:13:03.575 --> 00:13:04.855
fourth employee.

00:13:05.175 --> 00:13:07.735
So we're not overspent,

00:13:08.215 --> 00:13:11.735
we're just that money got allocated to a different bucket.

00:13:12.620 --> 00:13:16.620
The other thing we faced this year is that

00:13:16.780 --> 00:13:19.020
the $2,000

00:13:19.580 --> 00:13:20.620
that

00:13:20.620 --> 00:13:23.020
we overspent in public works,

00:13:23.260 --> 00:13:25.660
that equates to about 120.

00:13:26.305 --> 00:13:31.665
The total overtime budget for public works equates to about one hundred and twenty hours

00:13:32.225 --> 00:13:33.345
of labor.

00:13:34.305 --> 00:13:35.265
We used

00:13:36.225 --> 00:13:37.665
quite a bit of that

00:13:38.225 --> 00:13:40.545
on storm Aisa doing cleanup

00:13:40.890 --> 00:13:42.250
because we were,

00:13:42.890 --> 00:13:44.410
you know, we had them in

00:13:44.890 --> 00:13:46.570
all weekend essentially,

00:13:46.570 --> 00:13:48.250
two days during the weekend,

00:13:48.570 --> 00:13:50.890
and then over time several nights.

00:13:51.770 --> 00:13:53.850
We've also had a total

00:13:54.090 --> 00:13:56.090
of five events

00:13:56.225 --> 00:14:02.465
I needed to call the crew in because right now every time it's windy we got so many dead trees

00:14:02.625 --> 00:14:09.665
that we're ending up with road closures almost every time it's windy, which has been a lot this fall as many of you know.

00:14:09.985 --> 00:14:10.305
Again,

00:14:11.520 --> 00:14:17.280
the salary line item overall for Public Works is not overspent by any means.

00:14:17.600 --> 00:14:20.000
In fact we're under budget overall,

00:14:20.080 --> 00:14:22.000
so we're in good shape right now,

00:14:22.560 --> 00:14:25.920
but later in the year we are going to go over budget because

00:14:26.085 --> 00:14:28.405
Jay Tuttle has a, you know, a

00:14:28.805 --> 00:14:31.285
quite a bit higher salary than Eddie,

00:14:32.725 --> 00:14:34.485
but no overtime. So

00:14:35.125 --> 00:14:35.925
anyway,

00:14:36.245 --> 00:14:37.525
we'll be able to make it work.

00:14:38.260 --> 00:14:44.500
It's not going to be a huge deal in this budget, but it is something we're going to have to think about.

00:14:44.820 --> 00:14:47.140
I talked about the storm call outs.

00:14:49.140 --> 00:14:50.660
So again, the

00:14:51.135 --> 00:14:54.735
public work salary line item right now is 39%

00:14:54.735 --> 00:14:55.855
expended.

00:14:56.095 --> 00:14:59.695
That's the salary plus temp salary plus overtime.

00:15:00.175 --> 00:15:02.335
And we're already almost

00:15:02.335 --> 00:15:03.295
50%

00:15:03.295 --> 00:15:05.455
of the way through the budget cycle.

00:15:05.830 --> 00:15:07.510
So overall we're good,

00:15:07.830 --> 00:15:12.630
but two of those three categories are over, one is considerably under.

00:15:14.790 --> 00:15:18.310
So we have a significant snow event tonight.

00:15:18.975 --> 00:15:21.775
And just so you all know, to put it in perspective,

00:15:22.255 --> 00:15:28.815
I would anticipate this storm is going to cost the town a total of around sixty hours of overtime

00:15:28.895 --> 00:15:37.120
by the time we're all done with that, and probably another ten to twelve hours of help by one of our temps, Mark Williams.

00:15:37.200 --> 00:15:42.640
So we could expect a total storm cost for this of about $3,000

00:15:43.215 --> 00:15:44.975
not including salt,

00:15:46.735 --> 00:15:49.135
just to give you a ballpark of where we are.

00:15:50.495 --> 00:15:52.415
So finance department

00:15:55.810 --> 00:15:59.650
the town of Andover has hired the assistant finance office

00:15:59.890 --> 00:16:03.570
officer, sorry. Her name is Marina Pandolfi

00:16:03.970 --> 00:16:05.010
and

00:16:05.010 --> 00:16:05.890
she's

00:16:06.690 --> 00:16:07.890
working out really well.

00:16:08.805 --> 00:16:15.125
I've had her training pretty extensively with Laura who's the business manager for AES

00:16:15.445 --> 00:16:18.245
and also somewhat with Barbara.

00:16:19.765 --> 00:16:20.405
She

00:16:21.125 --> 00:16:22.965
does make considerably

00:16:22.965 --> 00:16:23.605
more

00:16:23.990 --> 00:16:26.230
than the previous

00:16:26.230 --> 00:16:27.110
assistant

00:16:27.190 --> 00:16:28.150
treasurer,

00:16:29.030 --> 00:16:31.190
but where she is in salary

00:16:31.510 --> 00:16:34.470
is kind of at the low end of the comparable

00:16:34.710 --> 00:16:35.510
compensation

00:16:35.510 --> 00:16:37.590
scale for somebody in her

00:16:37.830 --> 00:16:38.630
job position.

00:16:39.715 --> 00:16:41.075
That department,

00:16:42.115 --> 00:16:47.715
you know, total we're going to end up decreasing it from three people to two

00:16:47.955 --> 00:16:51.475
over between the last budget cycle and this budget cycle.

00:16:51.795 --> 00:16:58.310
So in the end, we're going to end up roughly the same amount of money, probably a little bit less total spending,

00:16:58.790 --> 00:17:06.550
but you know spending it on two higher level people rather than three people that were not paid as much.

00:17:08.995 --> 00:17:11.395
Probably a lot of you remember last year,

00:17:12.595 --> 00:17:14.755
we had a mix up with

00:17:14.755 --> 00:17:15.955
my salary.

00:17:16.515 --> 00:17:19.555
Basically when I first took the job,

00:17:20.595 --> 00:17:22.595
the old assistant treasurer,

00:17:24.230 --> 00:17:25.110
not

00:17:25.430 --> 00:17:28.870
realizing it was already almost September,

00:17:29.190 --> 00:17:30.470
took my

00:17:30.630 --> 00:17:35.350
yearly salary and divided it by the number of months remaining,

00:17:35.350 --> 00:17:37.030
not divided it by 12.

00:17:37.750 --> 00:17:38.870
I didn't catch it.

00:17:39.795 --> 00:17:42.755
We caught it basically at the end of last year

00:17:43.155 --> 00:17:44.915
because it seemed I

00:17:44.915 --> 00:17:45.795
was making

00:17:46.115 --> 00:17:48.115
more money than I should.

00:17:48.435 --> 00:17:52.035
And that really was the case. So what I did was

00:17:52.180 --> 00:17:57.060
I took the first three pay periods of this fiscal year off completely

00:17:57.220 --> 00:18:01.860
and just didn't get a salary. And then I've taken the rest of the money out

00:18:02.420 --> 00:18:04.740
of just

00:18:04.035 --> 00:18:11.555
basically cut my salary by that percentage for the remaining months of this year. So I'm basically back to neutral,

00:18:11.955 --> 00:18:14.115
but out of this budget cycle,

00:18:14.355 --> 00:18:15.875
I will have underspent

00:18:15.875 --> 00:18:17.395
the town administrator's

00:18:17.395 --> 00:18:19.875
budget by around $12,000

00:18:20.060 --> 00:18:23.580
So we'll be able to put that in either the finance officer

00:18:23.820 --> 00:18:26.220
or the public works supervisor

00:18:26.220 --> 00:18:30.620
line item. So I guess what I'm saying is overall we're fine on salary.

00:18:30.860 --> 00:18:33.820
We're going to be over in a few spots and under in a few spots.

00:18:34.945 --> 00:18:35.905
Additionally,

00:18:35.985 --> 00:18:39.185
last year I approached the board and the board agreed

00:18:39.585 --> 00:18:40.145
to

00:18:40.865 --> 00:18:43.105
hire a senior coordinator.

00:18:43.665 --> 00:18:46.705
I have held off on filling that position

00:18:47.540 --> 00:18:49.540
because frankly with COVID,

00:18:50.020 --> 00:18:53.940
we have such little capability to provide programming.

00:18:54.260 --> 00:18:56.020
It just didn't seem like

00:18:56.420 --> 00:18:59.620
now was the time to hire somebody into that position.

00:19:00.415 --> 00:19:05.935
I do feel strongly that that position is one we will want to fill,

00:19:06.255 --> 00:19:10.815
but I don't really want to think about filling it until

00:19:10.975 --> 00:19:12.495
such time as

00:19:13.490 --> 00:19:23.570
we're in a place where we can actually hold functions and actually program stuff for the seniors. So I'm letting you know we're going to be considerably under budget on that position

00:19:26.585 --> 00:19:27.785
going forward.

00:19:28.345 --> 00:19:30.345
So the town hall is open.

00:19:30.745 --> 00:19:33.225
We are what I would call semi staffed.

00:19:34.105 --> 00:19:35.865
We're not open to the public.

00:19:36.425 --> 00:19:38.185
And at this point, we have

00:19:39.060 --> 00:19:42.980
one employee that's tested positive for COVID-nineteen,

00:19:43.300 --> 00:19:46.100
and there's several others that are sick that are

00:19:46.820 --> 00:19:51.460
awaiting test results that could very well be COVID positive also.

00:19:52.055 --> 00:19:56.055
So we are moving some offices to remote staffing.

00:19:56.455 --> 00:19:59.335
We're keeping the staffing levels that we can,

00:19:59.735 --> 00:20:00.295
but

00:20:00.775 --> 00:20:02.855
we definitely have some people out,

00:20:03.335 --> 00:20:05.575
and we're going be working remote in some offices.

00:20:08.240 --> 00:20:12.320
As many of you remember, we had an IT security issue

00:20:12.960 --> 00:20:13.920
related

00:20:14.000 --> 00:20:14.640
to

00:20:15.120 --> 00:20:15.440
the

00:20:16.400 --> 00:20:18.800
our audit and our audit payment.

00:20:19.520 --> 00:20:21.520
One of the things that I implemented

00:20:22.375 --> 00:20:26.695
as a result of that was an IT security training program.

00:20:26.935 --> 00:20:30.055
So we started that for all the staff members.

00:20:30.375 --> 00:20:33.015
And it's something called Security Mentor.

00:20:33.255 --> 00:20:35.815
And it's basically just a series of online

00:20:36.170 --> 00:20:37.370
trainings

00:20:37.370 --> 00:20:39.050
in data security,

00:20:39.050 --> 00:20:41.770
office security, information security,

00:20:43.370 --> 00:20:46.650
everything to do with electronic transfer of information

00:20:46.890 --> 00:20:48.090
to hopefully

00:20:48.650 --> 00:20:50.490
prevent that from occurring again.

00:20:51.755 --> 00:20:56.475
And I did give a gift certificate to the first person to complete all the trainings.

00:20:58.155 --> 00:20:59.835
The other thing that's

00:20:59.995 --> 00:21:03.675
a very recent change is the building official

00:21:03.675 --> 00:21:04.795
has resigned.

00:21:06.050 --> 00:21:09.090
We're looking at our options. We have a couple different

00:21:09.490 --> 00:21:11.410
well we have three possibilities

00:21:11.410 --> 00:21:14.210
for where we want to go with the building official.

00:21:14.930 --> 00:21:20.370
And part of it is going to be a decision on our part whether we want to do it as a shared service

00:21:20.965 --> 00:21:23.045
provided by the town of Bolton.

00:21:24.085 --> 00:21:25.365
Bolton currently

00:21:25.365 --> 00:21:28.245
is themselves in three other towns,

00:21:28.485 --> 00:21:30.565
so they already provide

00:21:30.885 --> 00:21:32.165
building official

00:21:32.405 --> 00:21:34.405
or building department services

00:21:34.405 --> 00:21:35.685
for multiple towns.

00:21:37.100 --> 00:21:41.180
So that's one option. The other option would be just hiring

00:21:41.900 --> 00:21:46.540
a salaried position as a building official, a part time building official.

00:21:46.700 --> 00:21:50.300
We have two other individuals that are interested in doing that.

00:21:52.375 --> 00:21:52.935
So

00:21:53.495 --> 00:21:59.175
that is going to be more expensive than our existing arrangement with the old building official.

00:21:59.815 --> 00:22:02.935
However, the old building official has been

00:22:03.670 --> 00:22:04.710
resisting

00:22:04.790 --> 00:22:07.590
very heavily the last four to five years,

00:22:07.990 --> 00:22:11.430
bringing our fee structure in line with that of other towns.

00:22:11.830 --> 00:22:15.670
And we've gotten to the point where now building departments typically

00:22:15.830 --> 00:22:17.510
are basically self supporting.

00:22:18.125 --> 00:22:23.725
And we've gotten to the point where our building department was only taking in somewhere around 50%,

00:22:23.725 --> 00:22:25.085
55%

00:22:25.325 --> 00:22:27.725
of their costs in fees,

00:22:28.765 --> 00:22:32.285
because frankly, we were charging a lot less than surrounding

00:22:32.285 --> 00:22:32.765
towns.

00:22:33.790 --> 00:22:34.510
So

00:22:34.750 --> 00:22:35.630
we've

00:22:35.630 --> 00:22:36.430
undergone

00:22:36.430 --> 00:22:40.270
a review of all our building department fees,

00:22:40.750 --> 00:22:46.110
and we're probably over the next month or month and a half going to roll out a new fee schedule

00:22:46.590 --> 00:22:49.070
and a new way of calculating the fees.

00:22:49.575 --> 00:22:52.375
So in the end, I think we're going to

00:22:53.175 --> 00:22:55.255
get the building department

00:22:55.495 --> 00:22:56.775
to capture

00:22:56.775 --> 00:22:59.575
a much greater fraction of their total fees.

00:23:00.055 --> 00:23:00.855
Certainly

00:23:00.855 --> 00:23:03.895
our goal is to capture more than 80% of the fees

00:23:05.190 --> 00:23:09.190
or the expenses of the department in fees, which I think is completely

00:23:09.590 --> 00:23:10.470
realistic.

00:23:10.950 --> 00:23:14.550
So although we're going to end up paying more for a building official,

00:23:14.790 --> 00:23:16.070
the department overall

00:23:17.285 --> 00:23:24.005
will be less expensive to the town than it is now. Right now, I think frankly, we're subsidizing

00:23:24.085 --> 00:23:27.285
the cost of a lot of building and builders in town

00:23:28.005 --> 00:23:29.205
because

00:23:30.290 --> 00:23:35.170
we're not really passing through the costs of all the inspections and the services we provide.

00:23:36.530 --> 00:23:38.290
Any questions up to this point?

00:23:41.410 --> 00:23:43.570
>> Modernization going to include a

00:23:44.370 --> 00:23:45.570
building permit

00:23:46.185 --> 00:23:52.425
application that doesn't look like a photocopy of a photocopy of a photocopy that got skewed in the copier?

00:23:55.465 --> 00:24:00.105
We're going to Have change gone through the process recently myself building the shed in my property?

00:24:01.630 --> 00:24:06.430
>> Yeah, so I think we need to look at all the forms we have.

00:24:10.910 --> 00:24:11.790
Honestly,

00:24:11.790 --> 00:24:17.495
that's been a struggle that I tried to take on about a year and a half ago when I first came in,

00:24:17.975 --> 00:24:23.735
but the building official was fighting me pretty aggressively on that. So

00:24:26.375 --> 00:24:30.615
yes, we are looking at our fee schedule, we're looking at the way we

00:24:31.130 --> 00:24:33.450
give information to the public.

00:24:33.850 --> 00:24:37.450
And the one thing I think we will consider in the next

00:24:39.530 --> 00:24:41.050
year to two years

00:24:41.370 --> 00:24:42.170
is

00:24:42.890 --> 00:24:45.770
consider going to an online permitting system.

00:24:46.385 --> 00:24:48.385
They are not inexpensive.

00:24:50.705 --> 00:24:56.625
Most of the towns around us have gone to online permitting systems, not all of them, but most.

00:25:00.740 --> 00:25:01.780
So I

00:25:03.460 --> 00:25:08.980
think we can capture enough fees to make it fairly cost neutral to the town.

00:25:09.700 --> 00:25:13.460
It will be more expense on the part of builders.

00:25:14.045 --> 00:25:17.005
But when you think about it, what we're only talking about

00:25:17.325 --> 00:25:18.125
is

00:25:19.885 --> 00:25:25.645
for a normal town, a normal town, roughly 1.5%

00:25:26.080 --> 00:25:32.480
of the cost of a renovation or a new building is the permitting fees.

00:25:32.800 --> 00:25:36.720
So, you know, we're not talking about a radical increase

00:25:37.120 --> 00:25:37.760
to,

00:25:37.920 --> 00:25:43.755
I mean, permit fees are going to go way up, but in terms of actual project costs for developers

00:25:43.755 --> 00:25:49.995
it's not that much of a jump and it's relatively in line with what other towns are doing.

00:25:52.075 --> 00:25:53.275
Senior social

00:25:53.820 --> 00:26:00.700
>> shoot your question. Yeah, Eric, I just, you know, do we have any concern here where if we jack up the costs of

00:26:01.420 --> 00:26:05.820
doing these sorts of things that we might depress the amount of construction going on in town?

00:26:08.225 --> 00:26:09.425
>> I think

00:26:10.225 --> 00:26:12.465
if people are being honest,

00:26:13.105 --> 00:26:16.225
we're talking about a change from basically

00:26:16.625 --> 00:26:17.505
1%

00:26:17.505 --> 00:26:21.270
of the construction costs to about 1.5%

00:26:21.270 --> 00:26:22.950
of the construction costs.

00:26:23.110 --> 00:26:25.750
So the actual percentage difference

00:26:26.230 --> 00:26:27.670
cost in the job

00:26:28.070 --> 00:26:30.150
should be fairly miniscule

00:26:30.870 --> 00:26:33.670
if builders were being honest with us in the past.

00:26:34.415 --> 00:26:37.215
Okay. Which some are and some are not.

